#
# core=3.7: ATTACHMENTS. cascade:Attachment (new class) + hasAttachment,
#     attachmentPath, attachmentMediaType, contentHash, hashAlgorithm,
#     byteSize, attachmentTitle. All 8 terms implemented.
#     Attachment is a plain dataclass, NOT a CascadeRecord: it carries no
#     provenance and no schema version and cascade:AttachmentShape requires
#     neither, so subclassing would invent two required fields. It is written
#     as its OWN IRI-identified subject rather than an inline blank node,
#     because cascade:HasAttachmentEdgeShape asserts sh:nodeKind sh:IRI so the
#     record and the attachment can live in different files.
#     cascade:hasAttachment is domain-free ("any record that can be rendered as
#     a document"), so it sits on CascadeRecord rather than on one class.
#     The path, digest and algorithm patterns are enforced by the structural
#     validator, verbatim from the shape; media-type PRESENCE is a warning and
#     its FORM is an error, matching the two shapes upstream.
#
# health=2.8: NO TERM. The release adds none to health: — it gains three shape
#     bindings only (per-class clinical:status value sets). Version row moved
#     so downstream drift checks read true; nothing to implement.
#
# clinical=1.16: 15 terms (14 properties + 1 class). All implemented.
#     Encounter (9): encounterClassDisplay, encounterClassSystem (written
#     ^^xsd:anyURI, its declared range), encounterReason (repeatable),
#     admitSource, dischargeDisposition, hasParticipant, and the
#     EncounterParticipant class with participantName / participantRole /
#     participantRoleCode (repeatable) / participantSpecialty.
#     EncounterParticipant follows Attachment: plain dataclass, own IRI
#     subject. Its shape permits a blank node — nothing references a
#     participation from another file — but the conformance fixtures write an
#     IRI and Attachment's edge shape leaves no choice, so one treatment
#     serves both.
#     Identity (1): businessIdentifier, domain-free and repeatable, hence on
#     CascadeRecord. Values are the ratified "{system}|{value}" token form.
#     Distinct from sourceRecordId, which stays single-valued.
#     Documents (3): documentReferenceStatus, documentAuthorName,
#     authenticatorName are REGISTERED but NOT REACHABLE — clinical:
#     ClinicalDocument has no model in this SDK, so nothing writes or reads
#     them from a dataclass. They resolve through serialize_from_dict() and
#     the reverse predicate map; a document model is the open follow-up.
#
# coverage=1.5: coverage:status, the FHIR R4 fm-status codes. Implemented on
#     the Coverage dataclass. The bare field name "status" is bound to
#     health:status, so the coverage spelling is a type-specific serializer
#     override plus an explicit reverse mapping. The VALUE is an error (the
#     FHIR binding is REQUIRED and no pod has ever carried the property, so a
#     constraint cannot invalidate existing data); PRESENCE is deliberately not
#     required.
#
# Reader-side spellings registered by this sync (writers unchanged):
#     coverage:InsurancePlan as an rdf:type — CURRENT, not deprecated. It
#     resolved to no known type here, so such a subject was skipped in silence
#     by the reader AND the validator: a coverage fixture was not passing
#     validation, it was never being validated.
#     coverage:providerName / memberId / groupNumber / planName / planType /
#     coverageType / subscriberId, and clinical:sourceRecordId — every one a
#     property its vocabulary declares, and the spelling the fixtures carry.
#
# Also closed here because the v1.16 encounter fields could not otherwise be
# verified: clinical:Encounter is now registered in the deserializer's
# _TYPE_CLASS_MAP. It serialized correctly but parse() returned an EMPTY LIST,
# so all nine new fields would have been write-only and a round-trip test would
# have passed vacuously against zero records. The other types CLAUDE.md lists
# under "Deserializer registration" are untouched and still read as empty.

